Implement a deterministic phase scheduler in `solution.rs` using only the Rust standard library. It models elapsed time supplied by the caller; it must not access a system clock.

Expose this exact API:

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Phase { Warmup, Active, Finished }

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct PhaseMachine { pub phase: Phase, pub remaining_ms: u64 }

impl PhaseMachine {
    pub fn new(warmup_ms: u64, active_ms: u64) -> Self;
    pub fn advance(&mut self, elapsed_ms: u64) -> Vec<Phase>;
    pub fn is_finished(&self) -> bool;
}
```

`new` starts in `Warmup` with its duration. A zero-duration phase is crossed immediately during construction. Therefore, zero warmup starts in `Active`, and if active is also zero it starts in `Finished`. Store any duration needed for the later transition privately.

`advance` consumes elapsed time across any number of boundaries. It returns, in chronological order, every phase entered during that call; do not include the phase occupied at call entry. Exact exhaustion enters the next phase. Once finished, remaining time is zero, additional elapsed time is ignored, and `advance` returns an empty vector. `u64::MAX` inputs must not overflow. An `advance(0)` call causes no transition because construction has already normalized zero-duration phases.

